  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Pleasant/dry pattern continues into the last\nweekend of July\n\nValley fog continues to expand over the N&W Alleghenies per \nlatest IR satellite loop. Based on 07Z observations, some of \nthe usually colder sites in this area could print morning low \ntemps in the upper 30s. \n\nFog will quickly dissipate shortly after sunrise with temps \nalso rebounding nicely under abundant sunshine. The pleasant/dry\npattern continues into the last weekend of July. Sounding \nprofiles supported lowering Td this afternoon further reducing \nRH which is already quite comfortable for this time of year. \nHigh pressure migrates over CPA tonight setting up another \nseasonably cool/comfortable night with more valley fog into \nearly Friday morning. \n\nWind flow shifting from the north to the south signals a slight\nwarming trend and gradual increase in low level moisture Friday\nand Saturday. A stray shower or t-storm can't be ruled out\nSaturday afternoon/evening over western PA with precip odds less\nthan 20%. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: More humid with potential for strong thunderstorms\nand heavy rainfall into next week\n\nThe pattern looks increasingly unsettled Sunday into the middle\nof next week as upper troughing is carved out over the Great\nLakes/Ohio Valley into the Mid-Atlantic and Northeast states. \nAmple northern stream system energies set to dig into the upper \ntrough position will support potential for strong storms and\nheavy rainfall. WPC introduced a broad level 1/4 marginal \nexcessive rain risk for D5/Monday July 27 that covers most of \nCPA. Severe storm potential could shift east/southeast on\nTuesday. Confidence in where greater risk corridors may develop\nis low, and will largely be influenced by convective evolution \nin the Days 4-5/Sunday-Monday periods.",
